<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="512" height="512" viewBox="0 0 512 512" fill="currentColor">
<!-- CROSSED SPIKES, the mark for a HUMAN COMBAT (bounty / assault) task, drawn inside the
     task reticle rather than in a badge ring. Drawn here rather than sourced: two rhombi,
     four points each, straight edges only.

     IT REPLACED TWO RIFLE ROUNDS, kept at svg/bounty-rounds.alt, which had replaced
     CROSSED SWORDS (svg/bounty-swords.alt), which had replaced a bust
     (svg/bounty-bust.alt). Read bounty-swords.alt first: the constraint it states is
     still the one that governs this cell.

     THE CONSTRAINT, restated because it is the only thing that matters here. This mark's
     red (172,60,66) and the mutant hunt's lime (176,216,72) sit on the red-green axis,
     which is the axis red-green colour deficiency collapses. For those players SHAPE IS
     THE ONLY CHANNEL separating "a firefight" from "a mutant hunt". Any candidate has to
     be told apart from svg/skull.svg AT 26 PX WITH THE COLOUR REMOVED. Do not reason
     about the tints; render both cells to luminance and look.

     THIS IS THE SWORDS' ANSWER WITH THE SWORDS' DEFECT REMOVED. The X was never the
     problem - it is still the only silhouette class that is hollow in the middle with four
     limbs to the corners, against the skull's solid centred mass. The problem was that
     BLADES ARE THIN, so the mark carried too little ink and smudged at the size the map
     actually draws it. A spike is the same X with a fat middle: at the fit below it inks
     51% of the skull against the swords' 42% and the rounds' 45%, measured over the cell,
     and it is the crispest of the family at 18 px (the minimap's real size) because every
     edge is straight and there is no detail to lose.

     ASPECT IS THE OTHER HALF OF WHY IT WORKS. fit() sizes the LONG axis, so a landscape
     glyph gets a haircut on the short one: the rounds were 1.31:1 and the single pistol
     before them 1.53:1. This is 1.00:1 - it fills the reticle's inner box.

     RETICLE_FIT_SCALE IS 0.95 AND THAT IS A CEILING SET BY THE ARCS, not a spare knob.
     CORRECTING THE SWORDS NOTE, which claimed its tips at 1.15 were "still clear of the
     reticle arcs": THEY WERE NOT. THE GAPS ARE ON THE CARDINALS (TASK_GAP_DEG, and see the
     comment in draw_task), so the four arcs are CENTRED ON THE DIAGONALS - exactly where
     any crossed glyph points. There is no gap for a tip to pass through. The ring stroke
     spans 52.7 to 63.8 px from centre, and the swords reached 59.0: they were drawn
     touching the frame for three releases. An X spans its box corner to corner, so its
     reach is the half-diagonal, and clearing 52.7 with the 3 px a keyline needs caps the
     box at ~70 px. That is 0.95. Anything above it draws into the arcs; verify by
     measuring reach against clear_r, not by eye at 70 px, where the overlap hides.

     THE ONE THING IT DOES NOT SAY IS "HUMAN". It reads as crossed weapons - hostile
     combat - not as people, and that is a real cost against the bust that started this
     chain. It is accepted because the bust is unusable: a skull and a bust are the same
     silhouette at 26 px, which is the finding in bounty-swords.alt and the reason none of
     this can be solved by picking a better figure. Crossed rifles were built and measured
     as the alternative that does say "human" (1.03:1, 62% ink, an 8 deg splay to square
     the pair); they lose to this on the 18 px read and on having any detail at all to
     lose. If the mark must carry "human" some day, that is where to start.

     Filled rather than outline, per the measurement in skull.svg: at this size an outline
     glyph is mostly keyline, and a mark's apparent colour is the mean over its own area. -->
<path d="M131.5 380.5 L284.3 284.3 L380.5 131.5 L227.7 227.7Z
         M131.5 131.5 L227.7 284.3 L380.5 380.5 L284.3 227.7Z" />
</svg>
